WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.260
Six loose counters arrive

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.450
at two trays marked with the same letter.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.660
Split them equally.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.060
Three on this tray and three on its twin.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.040
A digit written twice must describe

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.020
the same quantity both times.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.360
Now each tray holds a two digit number

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.530
ending in five.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.870
Together their total begins with seven.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.890
Tip the two groups of five into the units drawer.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.020
All ten fit in a sleeve worth one ten.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.880
The units drawer is empty.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.440
Its digit is zero.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.350
The tens drawer shows seven sleeves.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.580
One just came across from the units.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.010
Take that one aside.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.520
Six sleeves remain,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.470
shared equally by the two trays.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.000
Each tray supplied three tens.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.850
Thirty five and thirty five make seventy.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.120
Turn the counter round.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.390
Two copies of the number A B

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.530
have made a three digit total labelled B C D.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.120
Different letters must have different digits.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.810
We want the smallest possible A B.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.580
Before opening the drawers,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.990
which letter can you name?

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.130
Even ninety nine doubled

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.250
is only one hundred and ninety eight.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.930
The hundreds drawer must hold exactly one,

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.330
so B is one.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.480
Move that digit back to both units places.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.640
One plus one leaves two units

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.550
and sends nothing into the tens.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.280
D is two.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.380
Four tens on each tray

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.100
would make only eight tens.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.730
Five on each tray make ten tens.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.890
Exchange them for one hundred.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.750
No tens remain, so C is zero.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.380
Read the finished drawers:

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.450
fifty one plus fifty one equals

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.610
one hundred and two.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.840
Five, one, zero and two are all different.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.310
No smaller tens digit can work.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.800
Now double sixty eight.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.830
Eight and eight make sixteen.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.750
Leave six in the units drawer

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.450
and send one ten across.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.950
Six tens and six tens plus the carried ten

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.350
make thirteen tens.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.040
Exchange ten of those tens for a hundred.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.730
The drawers read one hundred and thirty six.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.540
In a letter sum, keep the digit you write

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.310
separate from the amount you carry.
